<%- include('partials/head', { title: 'Change password' }) %>

<div class="card card-narrow">
  <h1>Change password</h1>
  <%- include('partials/flash') %>

  <form method="POST" action="/account/password" autocomplete="on">
    <label for="current_password">Current password</label>
    <input id="current_password" name="current_password" type="password" autocomplete="current-password" required>

    <label for="new_password">New password</label>
    <input id="new_password" name="new_password" type="password" autocomplete="new-password" required minlength="8">

    <button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">Update password</button>
  </form>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>Make sure 1Password catches this</h2>
    <p>Right after you submit, 1Password should pop up a banner asking to
      <strong>update</strong> the saved login. That's the moment that
      matters — click it. If you miss it, or dismiss it by accident,
      1Password is still holding the old password, and your next login
      attempt with the autofilled value will fail with "Incorrect username
      or password" here, even though the account itself changed.</p>
    <p>If that happens, you don't need to redo the change — just fix
      1Password's copy:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open 1Password and find this login (search for this site's
        address, or your username).</li>
      <li>Edit the entry and replace the password field with the new one
        you just set.</li>
      <li>Save, then try logging in again — it should autofill correctly
        this time.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="hint">This mismatch is common enough in real life that it's
      worth deliberately practicing the recovery, not just the happy path:
      change the password here, close the 1Password prompt without saving,
      then log out and try to log back in on purpose. You'll see the
      failed autofill, then walk through fixing the saved entry above.</p>
  </div>

  <p><a href="/account">Back to account</a></p>
</div>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
